## Introduction

>This report follows the required steps for a longitudinal social network analysis using stochastic actor-oriented models (SAOMs) implemented in RSiena. We analyze directed “best friends” nominations in a Dutch secondary school classroom across three waves (**V, X, Y**) from Knecht’s adolescent networks study (Knecht, 2004; https://doi.org/10.17026/DANS-Z9B-H2BP).
>
>We examine whether (i) sex homophily shapes best-friend nominations (dyadic hypothesis), (ii) triadic closure/transitivity drives clustering (triadic hypothesis), and (iii) a node-level popularity mechanism affects nominations (indegree-related popularity; `inPop`). We estimate an initial specification (Model 1) and then update it once based on goodness-of-fit diagnostics (Model 2, final). SAOM estimation is carried out on a constant node set (pupils observed in all three waves).



## 1. Network Descriptives

```{r}
#| label: setup-1
#| include: false
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false



library(haven)
library(tidyverse)
library(igraph)
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(tibble)
library(knitr)

# Helper to pick the correct variable name when it differs across waves
get_first_existing <- function(df, candidates) {
  cand <- candidates[candidates %in% names(df)]
  if (length(cand) == 0) stop("No variable found. Checked: ", paste(candidates, collapse = ", "))
  cand[1]
}





data_dir <- "."

waveV_path <- file.path(data_dir, "PupilsWaveV.sav")
waveW_path <- file.path(data_dir, "PupilsWaveW_geanonimiseerd.sav")
waveX_path <- file.path(data_dir, "PupilsWaveX.sav")
waveY_path <- file.path(data_dir, "PupilsWaveY.sav")

waveV <- read_sav(waveV_path)
waveW <- read_sav(waveW_path)
waveX <- read_sav(waveX_path)
waveY <- read_sav(waveY_path)
```

```{r}
#| label: select-class
#| echo: false
class_id <- "09f"

classV <- subset(waveV, schoolnr == class_id)
classX <- subset(waveX, schoolnr == class_id)
classY <- subset(waveY, schoolnr == class_id)
```


> We focus on classroom **`r class_id`** (variable `schoolnr`). This classroom was selected because it provides at least three observed waves and shows sufficient stability (composition and tie Jaccard) to support SAOM estimation while still exhibiting meaningful change over time.
Although the original dataset contains four survey waves (W, V, X, Y), we restrict the analysis to waves V, X, and Y. Including wave W would reduce the set of pupils observed in all waves (a constant node set is required for SAOM estimation), which would substantially shrink the network used in the longitudinal model. Therefore, we focus on the three waves that preserve sufficient overlap and comparability for SAOM estimation.



```{r}
#| label: create-network-function
#| echo: false
# Function to transform nominations (friend1..friend12) into an adjacency matrix.
#  codes 98/99 indicate empty nomination slots (no further nominations) and should NOT become edges.
create_network <- function(df, friend_cols, id_col = "namenr", miss_codes = c(98, 99)) {
  ids <- as.numeric(df[[id_col]])
  n <- length(ids)
  mat <- matrix(0L, n, n, dimnames = list(ids, ids))

  for (i in seq_len(n)) {
    nominations <- unlist(df[i, friend_cols], use.names = FALSE) |> as.numeric()
    nominations <- nominations[!is.na(nominations)]
    nominations <- nominations[!nominations %in% miss_codes]

    # Keep only nominations that correspond to classmates present in this wave
    nominations <- unique(nominations[nominations %in% ids])

    if (length(nominations) > 0) {
      mat[i, match(nominations, ids)] <- 1L
    }
  }

  diag(mat) <- 0L
  mat
}
```

```{r}
#| label: friend-columns
#| echo: false
# Wave V
friend_cols_V <- paste0("friend", 1:12)

# Wave X
friend_cols_X <- paste0("frien", 1:12, "c")

# Wave Y
friend_cols_Y <- paste0("frien", 1:12, "d")
```

```{r}
#| label: build-networks
#| echo: false
# Networks per wave (each wave may have a slightly different set of students)
netV <- create_network(classV, friend_cols_V)
netX <- create_network(classX, friend_cols_X)
netY <- create_network(classY, friend_cols_Y)

gV <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netV, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)
gX <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netX, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)
gY <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netY, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)

# Density (proportion of existing ties among all possible ties)
densities <- c(edge_density(gV), edge_density(gX), edge_density(gY))
nodes <- c(vcount(gV), vcount(gX), vcount(gY))

summary_table <- tibble(
  Wave = c("Wave V", "Wave X", "Wave Y"),
  Nodes = nodes,
  Density = densities
) |>
  mutate(
    DensityChangePct = c(NA_real_,
                        (Density[2] - Density[1]) / Density[1] * 100,
                        (Density[3] - Density[2]) / Density[2] * 100)
  )
```

> @fig-network-density summarizes network density across waves (using the observed classroom composition in each wave).

```{r}
#| label: fig-network-density
#| fig-cap: "Network density across waves"
#| echo: false
ggplot(summary_table, aes(x = Wave, y = Density)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_text(aes(label = round(Density, 3)), vjust = -0.5) +
  ylim(0, max(summary_table$Density) + 0.03) +
  labs(title = "Network density across waves", y = "Density", x = NULL) +
  theme_minimal()
```

> These values are proportions of possible friendships that exist. So for Wave V, only about `r round(summary_table$Density[1] * 100, 1)`% of all possible ties were actually reported.
Wave X is slightly denser (about `r round(summary_table$Density[2] * 100, 1)`%), and Wave Y is very similar (about `r round(summary_table$Density[3] * 100, 1)`%).
Networks with density \< 0.2 are considered sparse, which is very common in social networks because not everyone is friends with everyone else. @tbl-network-descriptives reports basic descriptives of the best-friends network across waves.

```{r}
#| label: tbl-network-descriptives
#| tbl-cap: "Basic descriptives of the best-friends network across waves (observed classroom composition per wave)"
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
knitr::kable(summary_table, digits = 3)
```


>Across the three waves, classroom composition changes slightly (30 pupils in V, 28 in X, 26 in Y). Network density is low (0.145–0.177), which is typical for “best friends” nomination networks.

>SAOM estimation requires a constant set of actors. Therefore, after describing the observed networks above, we (i) quantify composition stability using the node-set Jaccard index, (ii) quantify tie turnover using the tie Jaccard index computed on a constant node set, and (iii) restrict SAOM estimation to pupils observed in all three waves (the intersection sample).

>Missing or empty nomination slots: Each respondent could nominate up to 12 classmates. Nomination entries coded as 98/99 are treated as no nomination, empty slot and are not converted into ties. Nominations to IDs not present in the classroom roster of that wave are discarded. All remaining non-nominated dyads are coded as 0 ties.


```{r}
#| label: nodeset-stability-setup
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
# Stability of the node set (classroom composition) between waves
jaccard_index <- function(a, b) {
  length(intersect(a, b)) / length(union(a, b))
}

j_node_VX <- jaccard_index(classV$namenr, classX$namenr)
j_node_XY <- jaccard_index(classX$namenr, classY$namenr)

jaccard_table <- tibble(
  Wave_Comparison = c("Wave V → Wave X", "Wave X → Wave Y"),
  Jaccard_NodeSet = round(c(j_node_VX, j_node_XY), 3)
)

# For SAOM (RSiena), need the SAME set of students in all waves.
# work with the intersection (students present in all three waves).
ids_common <- Reduce(intersect, list(classV$namenr, classX$namenr, classY$namenr)) |> sort()
ids_common_n <- length(ids_common)

# Align the data (same ID order) for SAOM
classV_saom <- classV |> filter(namenr %in% ids_common) |> arrange(match(namenr, ids_common))
classX_saom <- classX |> filter(namenr %in% ids_common) |> arrange(match(namenr, ids_common))
classY_saom <- classY |> filter(namenr %in% ids_common) |> arrange(match(namenr, ids_common))

netV_saom <- create_network(classV_saom, friend_cols_V)
netX_saom <- create_network(classX_saom, friend_cols_X)
netY_saom <- create_network(classY_saom, friend_cols_Y)
```

```{r}
#| label: tbl-jaccard-nodeset
#| tbl-cap: "Jaccard stability of the node set (classroom composition)"
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
knitr::kable(jaccard_table)
```

```{r}
#| label: tbl-intersection-count
#| tbl-cap: "Number of students present in all three waves (intersection)"
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
knitr::kable(tibble(Students_in_all_three_waves = ids_common_n))
```


```{r}
#| label: tbl-network-descriptives-intersection
#| tbl-cap: "Basic descriptives of the best-friends network on the constant node set (intersection sample)."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis

gV_saom <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netV_saom, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)
gX_saom <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netX_saom, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)
gY_saom <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netY_saom, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)

summary_table_saom <- tibble(
  Wave = c("Wave V", "Wave X", "Wave Y"),
  Nodes = c(vcount(gV_saom), vcount(gX_saom), vcount(gY_saom)),
  Density = c(edge_density(gV_saom), edge_density(gX_saom), edge_density(gY_saom))
)

knitr::kable(summary_table_saom, digits = 3)
```

> The intersection sample contains **`r ids_common_n`** pupils and is the sample used for SAOM estimation..


```{r}
#| label: tbl-jaccard-ties
#| tbl-cap: "Jaccard stability of ties on the constant node set (intersection sample)"
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
#| message: false
#| warning: false

# Jaccard stability for ties (friendship relations) between consecutive waves
jaccard_ties <- function(A, B) {
  A <- (A > 0); B <- (B > 0)
  diag(A) <- FALSE; diag(B) <- FALSE
  inter <- sum(A & B, na.rm = TRUE)
  uni   <- sum(A | B, na.rm = TRUE)
  if (uni == 0) return(NA_real_)
  inter / uni
}

j_tie_VX <- jaccard_ties(netV_saom, netX_saom)
j_tie_XY <- jaccard_ties(netX_saom, netY_saom)

tie_jaccard_table <- tibble(
  Wave_Comparison = c("Wave V → Wave X", "Wave X → Wave Y"),
  Jaccard_Ties    = round(c(j_tie_VX, j_tie_XY), 3)
)

knitr::kable(tie_jaccard_table)
```

> The table above reports tie Jaccard stability (overlapping friendships) between consecutive waves on the constant node set used for SAOM. Higher values indicate more stable friendship relations across time, supporting the use of longitudinal modelling.
The Jaccard indices above quantify how stable the classroom composition is across waves. Because SAOMs require a constant node set, the subsequent SAOM analysis will be run on the students who are present in all three waves (the intersection).


## 2. Dyadic Hypothesis


>For the analysis of dyadic characteristics in the “Best friends” network, we formulated the following hypothesis: students are more likely to nominate as best friends those peers who are of the same sex. This hypothesis is based on the well-established principle of sex homophily in adolescent social networks, where boys tend to form friendships with other boys, and girls with other girls. Theoretical considerations, such as social identity theory, suggest that individuals prefer closer ties with peers who share salient characteristics like sex, which can also facilitate participation in similar classroom activities or interests. Empirical studies in classroom social networks consistently report high levels of sex-based homophily (e.g., Snijders et al., 2010). To test sex homophily in RSiena, we use the `sameX` effect with the actor covariate `sex_cov`. A positive `sameX` parameter indicates that pupils are more likely to nominate peers of the same sex as best friends.

> As a descriptive complement without presenting large similarity matrices, we report the share of observed nominations that go to same-sex peers in each wave computed on the constant node set used in the SAOM.

```{r}
#| label: tbl-same-sex-ties
#| tbl-cap: "Share of same-sex best-friend nominations by wave (constant node set)."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis

# Build a sex vector aligned to the SAOM node order
sex_col_V_saom <- get_first_existing(classV_saom, c("sex", "sexc", "sexd"))
sex_col_X_saom <- get_first_existing(classX_saom, c("sexc", "sex", "sexd"))
sex_col_Y_saom <- get_first_existing(classY_saom, c("sexd", "sex", "sexc"))

sex_saom_num <- dplyr::coalesce(
  as.numeric(classV_saom[[sex_col_V_saom]]),
  as.numeric(classX_saom[[sex_col_X_saom]]),
  as.numeric(classY_saom[[sex_col_Y_saom]])
)

share_same_sex <- function(A, sex_vec) {
  same <- outer(sex_vec, sex_vec, FUN = "==")
  diag(same) <- NA
  ties <- (A > 0)
  num <- sum(ties & same, na.rm = TRUE)
  den <- sum(ties, na.rm = TRUE)
  if (den == 0) return(NA_real_)
  num / den
}

same_sex_tbl <- tibble(
  Wave = c("Wave V", "Wave X", "Wave Y"),
  Share_same_sex = c(
    share_same_sex(netV_saom, sex_saom_num),
    share_same_sex(netX_saom, sex_saom_num),
    share_same_sex(netY_saom, sex_saom_num)
  )
)

knitr::kable(same_sex_tbl, digits = 3)
```



## 3. Triadic Hypothesis



>For the analysis of triadic characteristics in the “Best friends” network, we formulated the following hypothesis: friendship ties are likely to exhibit transitivity, meaning that if student A nominates student B as a best friend, and student B nominates student C, then student A is also likely to nominate student C. This hypothesis is grounded in the principle of triadic closure, a common phenomenon in social networks, where “friends of friends tend to become friends” (Granovetter, 1973; Snijders et al., 2010). Theoretical explanations suggest that transitive ties increase trust, reduce social tension, and facilitate cooperation within a group. In classroom networks, transitivity is often observed because students share activities, classes, and social environments, making it more likely that friendship clusters form.
>To operationalize this triadic hypothesis in RSiena, we use the transTrip effect, which captures triadic closure directly within the SAOM. A positive transTrip parameter indicates that the presence of A->B and B->C increases the likelihood of A->C forming over time, reflecting clustering in the classroom network. As a descriptive check, we also compute and report global transitivity (triadic closure) for each wave on the constant node set used for the SAOM.

> @tbl-transitivity reports global transitivity (triadic closure) for each wave.

```{r}
#| label: transitivity-setup
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
# Compute global transitivity (triadic closure) for each wave
gV <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netV_saom, mode = "directed")
gX <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netX_saom, mode = "directed")
gY <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netY_saom, mode = "directed")

trans_V <- transitivity(gV, type = "global")
trans_X <- transitivity(gX, type = "global")
trans_Y <- transitivity(gY, type = "global")

trans_table <- tibble(
  Wave = c("Wave V", "Wave X", "Wave Y"),
  Transitivity = round(c(trans_V, trans_X, trans_Y), 3)
)
```

```{r}
#| label: tbl-transitivity
#| tbl-cap: "Global transitivity by wave"
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
knitr::kable(trans_table)
```

>The transitivity of the “Best friends” network, which measures the tendency of friends-of-friends to also be friends (triadic closure), shows a moderate to high level of clustering across the three waves. In Wave V, the transitivity is `r trans_table$Transitivity[1]`, indicating that a substantial share of possible triads are closed. Wave X exhibits a transitivity of `r trans_table$Transitivity[2]`, reflecting the tendency for friendship triads to form. In Wave Y, the transitivity is `r trans_table$Transitivity[3]`, suggesting that the level of triadic closure remains in a similar range over time. These results support the hypothesis that friendship formation in the classroom is characterized by transitive relationships, consistent with established findings in adolescent social networks where social groups tend to form tightly connected clusters

## 4. Node Attribute Hypothesis


>For the analysis of node-level characteristics in the “Best friends” network, we formulated the following hypothesis: students with higher levels of popularity (indegree) are more likely to be nominated as best friends by their peers. This hypothesis is based on the idea that individuals with high social status or visibility in the classroom attract more friendship nominations, consistent with the principle of preferential attachment in social networks. Empirical studies in adolescent classrooms indicate that popular students tend to receive more friendship ties and maintain them over time (e.g., Snijders et al., 2010). To operationalize this hypothesis, a popularity score was assigned to each student based on the total number of received best-friend nominations (indegree). This score can then be used to capture popularity dynamics in a SAOM (here via the inPop effect) to test whether higher popularity increases the likelihood of receiving additional nominations over time.


```{r}
#| label: pop-saom-setup
#| echo: false
#| message: false
#| warning: false

# Popularity on the constant node set used for SAOM
pop_V_saom <- colSums(netV_saom, na.rm = TRUE)
pop_X_saom <- colSums(netX_saom, na.rm = TRUE)
pop_Y_saom <- colSums(netY_saom, na.rm = TRUE)

pop_saom_df <- tibble(
  Wave = rep(c("Wave V", "Wave X", "Wave Y"), each = length(pop_V_saom)),
  Popularity = c(pop_V_saom, pop_X_saom, pop_Y_saom)
)

pop_saom_summary <- pop_saom_df %>%
  group_by(Wave) %>%
  summarise(
    Mean = mean(Popularity),
    Min  = min(Popularity),
    Max  = max(Popularity),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

```

```{r}
#| label: fig-mean-popularity
#| fig-cap: "Mean popularity (indegree) across waves, constant node set used for SAOM (n = `25`)."
#| echo: false
#| message: false
#| warning: false

ggplot(pop_saom_summary, aes(x = Wave, y = Mean)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_text(aes(label = round(Mean, 2)), vjust = -0.5) +
  ylim(0, max(pop_saom_summary$Mean) + 1) +
  labs(y = "Mean number of nominations received (indegree)", x = NULL) +
  theme_minimal()
```
```{r}
#| label: tbl-popularity-summary-saom
#| tbl-cap: "Popularity descriptives (indegree) by wave, constant node set used for SAOM."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis

knitr::kable(pop_saom_summary, digits = 2)
```
> For the node-level analysis, popularity was calculated as indegree, the total number of best-friend nominations each pupil received in each wave. Because SAOM estimation requires a constant node set, all popularity descriptives reported here are computed on the same intersection sample used in the model (n = 25). On this constant node set, mean popularity is 3.40 in Wave V, 4.60 in Wave X, and 4.16 in Wave Y (@fig-mean-popularity), suggesting a temporary increase in received nominations in Wave X followed by a slight decrease in Wave Y. The range of popularity also indicates heterogeneity in nomination concentration within the classroom, with minima and maxima of 0 to 6 in Wave V, 0 to 7 in Wave X, and 1 to 7 in Wave Y. 


## 5. Control Variables


> In our analysis of the “Best friends” network, several control variables were included to account for factors that might influence friendship formation and maintenance:

>Ego-level variables: These describe characteristics of the student sending the friendship nomination. For example, we include outdegree activity (outAct) to account for heterogeneity in pupils’ tendency to send nominations (i.e., some students nominate more peers than others).

>Alter-level variables: These describe characteristics of the student receiving the nomination. For instance, we include indegree-related popularity (inPop) to capture whether pupils who already receive many nominations become more/less likely to receive additional nominations over time (an endogenous degree-related mechanism).

>Dyadic variables: These variables describe pairwise relationships between students. For example, sex homophily is modeled via sameX using the actor covariate sex_cov, capturing the tendency for students to nominate same-sex peers as best friends.

>Structural/network variables: These account for endogenous network processes, such as outdegree (density), which captures the baseline sparsity of the network, reciprocity, which captures the tendency for a nomination to be returned by the alter, and transitivity (transTrip), which measures the tendency for friends of friends to become friends. 

>Together, these control variables allow the stochastic actor-oriented model to isolate the effects of individual, dyadic, and structural factors on the evolution of the friendship network over time.

>@tbl-controls lists the control variables used in the analysis..

```{r}
#| label: controls-setup
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false

library(tibble)

control_vars <- tibble(
  Variable = c(
    "Outdegree (density)",
    "Reciprocity",
    "Sex homophily (sameX)",
    "Transitivity (transTrip)",
    "Indegree-related popularity (inPop)",
    "Outdegree activity (outAct)",
    "Reciprocity activity (reciAct)"   # 
  ),
  Type = c(
    "Structural (baseline)",
    "Dyadic/structural (endogenous)",
    "Dyadic (based on actor covariate)",
    "Triadic/structural (endogenous)",
    "Degree-related / endogenous",
    "Degree-related / endogenous",
    "Degree-related / endogenous"
  ),
  Description = c(
    "Baseline tendency toward a sparse nomination network.",
    "Tendency to reciprocate a best-friend nomination.",
    "Preference for same-sex nominations (computed from sex_cov via sameX).",
    "Friends-of-friends closure: A→B and B→C increases A→C.",
    "Whether higher-indegree pupils become more/less likely to receive additional nominations over time (endogenous popularity).",
    "Heterogeneity in pupils' tendency to send nominations beyond baseline effects.",
    "Heterogeneity related to reciprocating nominations; added to improve outdegree fit (Model 2)."
  )
)
```


```{r}
#| label: tbl-controls
#| tbl-cap: "Control variables to be included in the analysis"
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
knitr::kable(control_vars)
```

## 6. Longitudinal Plots


>We visualize the best-friends network over time (waves V, X, Y) using fixed node positions and node colors by popularity (indegree; number of nominations received). Popularity is recalculated in each wave on the constant node set used for SAOM. Fixed positions ensure that changes in ties and in node popularity across waves can be compared directly without layout-induced movement of nodes.
> @fig-network-wave-v, @fig-network-wave-x, and @fig-network-wave-y visualize the best-friends network in Waves V, X, and Y using fixed node positions and node colors by popularity (indegree).

```{r}
#| label: q6-setup
#| include: false

library(igraph)
library(ggraph)
library(dplyr)
library(grid)

# Build igraph objects from SAOM-aligned adjacency matrices (same node set in all waves)
gV_saom <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netV_saom, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)
gX_saom <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netX_saom, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)
gY_saom <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netY_saom, mode = "directed", diag = FALSE)

# Compute ONE fixed layout from the aggregated network (undirected for stable layout)
netAgg <- ((netV_saom + netX_saom + netY_saom) > 0) * 1
gAgg <- graph_from_adjacency_matrix(netAgg, mode = "undirected", diag = FALSE)

set.seed(123)
xy <- layout_with_fr(gAgg)

# Node color attribute: popularity (indegree = nominations received) per wave
pop_V <- colSums(netV_saom)
pop_X <- colSums(netX_saom)
pop_Y <- colSums(netY_saom)

# Keep the same color scale across waves for comparability
pop_lim <- range(c(pop_V, pop_X, pop_Y), na.rm = TRUE)

V(gV_saom)$pop <- pop_V
V(gX_saom)$pop <- pop_X
V(gY_saom)$pop <- pop_Y

plot_wave_pop <- function(g, xy, pop_lim) {
  ggraph(g, layout = "manual", x = xy[, 1], y = xy[, 2]) +
    geom_edge_link(
      alpha = 0.35,
      arrow = grid::arrow(length = grid::unit(1.8, "mm"))
    ) +
    geom_node_point(aes(color = pop), size = 3) +
    scale_color_continuous(limits = pop_lim) +
    labs(color = "Popularity\n(indegree)") +
    theme_void()
}

```

```{r}
#| label: fig-network-wave-v
#| fig-cap: "Best-friends network in Wave V (fixed node positions; node color indicates popularity/indegree)."
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 4.6

plot_wave_pop(gV_saom, xy, pop_lim)

```



```{r}
#| label: fig-network-wave-x
#| fig-cap: "Best-friends network in Wave X (fixed node positions; node color indicates popularity/indegree)."
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 4.6

plot_wave_pop(gX_saom, xy, pop_lim)



```


```{r}
#| label: fig-network-wave-y
#| fig-cap: "Best-friends network in Wave Y (fixed node positions; node color indicates popularity/indegree)."
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 4.6

plot_wave_pop(gY_saom, xy, pop_lim)

```



## 7. Model Specification


>In this project, we test three hypotheses using a stochastic actor-oriented model (SAOM) in RSiena. We operationalize each hypothesis by selecting the corresponding RSiena effect(s). @tbl-saom-operationalization shows how each hypothesis is translated into a SAOM (RSiena) effect.

```{r}
#| label: tbl-saom-operationalization
#| tbl-cap: "Operationalization of hypotheses as RSiena effects."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis

saom_map <- tibble::tibble(
  Hypothesis = c(
    "H1 (Dyadic): sex homophily",
    "H2 (Triadic): transitivity",
    "H3 (Node-level): popularity reinforcement"
  ),
  RSiena_effect = c(
    "sameX (interaction1 = sex_cov)",
    "transTrip",
    "inPop (indegree popularity)"
  ),
  Expc_direc = c(
    "Positive",
    "Positive",
    "Positive"
  ),
  Interpretation = c(
    "Ties are more likely between pupils of the same sex.",
    "Friend-of-a-friend closure: A→B and B→C increases A→C.",
    "Pupils who already receive many nominations are more likely to receive additional nominations over time."
  )
)

knitr::kable(saom_map)

```



>Sex homophily is modeled with the `sameX` effect using the sex covariate (`interaction1 = "sex_cov"`). A positive parameter indicates that pupils are more likely to nominate same-sex peers as best friends. Transitivity is captured by `transTrip`, where a positive parameter indicates triadic closure (“friends of friends become friends”). Our node-level hypothesis about popularity is operationalized with `inPop` (indegree popularity), an endogenous effect that tests whether pupils who already receive many nominations tend to attract additional nominations over time. Finally, we include `outAct` (outdegree activity) as an endogenous structural control to account for heterogeneity in pupils’ tendency to send nominations beyond the focal hypotheses.



## 8. Model Estimation

>We estimate SAOMs in RSiena on the constant node set (intersection sample; 25) using the aligned adjacency matrices (`netV_saom`, `netX_saom`, `netY_saom`). RSiena’s default network objective function already includes baseline structural terms (outdegree/density and reciprocity).
>
>Model 1 adds our hypothesis-driven effects (`sameX(sex_cov)`, `transTrip`, `inPop`) and a standard degree-heterogeneity control (`outAct`). If convergence is not reached, we increase estimation effort (`n3`) and continue from previous estimates (`prevAns`).
>Based on GoF diagnostics, we updated the specification by adding reciAct.




```{r}
#| label: siena-fit-models
#| echo: false
#| results: hide
#| message: false
#| warning: false

library(RSiena)
library(dplyr)

# Dependent network: 3 waves
friendship_saom <- sienaDependent(
  array(c(netV_saom, netX_saom, netY_saom),
        dim = c(nrow(netV_saom), ncol(netV_saom), 3))
)

# Constant actor covariate: sex (used for sameX)
sex_col_V_saom <- get_first_existing(classV_saom, c("sex", "sexc", "sexd"))
sex_col_X_saom <- get_first_existing(classX_saom, c("sexc", "sex", "sexd"))
sex_col_Y_saom <- get_first_existing(classY_saom, c("sexd", "sex", "sexc"))

sex_saom_num <- dplyr::coalesce(
  as.numeric(classV_saom[[sex_col_V_saom]]),
  as.numeric(classX_saom[[sex_col_X_saom]]),
  as.numeric(classY_saom[[sex_col_Y_saom]])
)

sex_cov <- coCovar(sex_saom_num)

# RSiena data
siena_dat <- sienaDataCreate(friendship_saom, sex_cov)

# Effects (Model 1): defaults + hypotheses + one control
eff_m1 <- getEffects(siena_dat)

# H2: transitivity
eff_m1 <- includeEffects(eff_m1, transTrip)

# H1: sex homophily
eff_m1 <- includeEffects(eff_m1, sameX, interaction1 = "sex_cov")

# H3: popularity reinforcement (indegree popularity)
eff_m1 <- includeEffects(eff_m1, inPop)

# Structural control: heterogeneity in sending nominations
eff_m1 <- includeEffects(eff_m1, outAct)

# Algorithm + estimation (Model 1)
alg1 <- sienaAlgorithmCreate(projname = "bestfriends_saom_VXY", seed = 12345)
res_m1 <- siena07(alg1, data = siena_dat, effects = eff_m1, batch = TRUE, silent = TRUE)

# If not converged, continue with more effort
alg2 <- sienaAlgorithmCreate(projname = "bestfriends_saom_VXY", seed = 12345, n3 = 4000)
res_m1b <- siena07(alg2, data = siena_dat, effects = eff_m1, prevAns = res_m1,
                   batch = TRUE, silent = TRUE)

# Choose final
# Model 2: add reciprocity activity (reciAct)
eff_m2 <- eff_m1
eff_m2 <- includeEffects(eff_m2, reciAct)

alg_m2 <- sienaAlgorithmCreate(projname = "bestfriends_saom_VXY_m2", seed = 12345, n3 = 4000)
res_m2 <- siena07(
  alg_m2,
  data = siena_dat,
  effects = eff_m2,
  prevAns = res_m1b,
  batch = TRUE,
  silent = TRUE
)

# Choose final (Model 2)
res_final <- res_m2
```



> @tbl-siena-results reports the parameter estimates for the final converged model (Model 2).


```{r}
#| label: tbl-convergence
#| tbl-cap: "Convergence diagnostics for the final estimation run."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
#| message: false
#| warning: false



conv_tbl <- tibble(
  Metric = c("tconv.max", "max(|tconv|)"),
  Value  = c(
    res_final$tconv.max,
    max(abs(res_final$tconv), na.rm = TRUE)
  )
)

knitr::kable(conv_tbl, digits = 3)
```

```{r}
#| label: tbl-rates
#| tbl-cap: "Rate parameters (opportunities for change) between waves."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
#| message: false
#| warning: false

library(tibble)
library(knitr)

rate_tbl <- tibble(
  Period = c("V → X", "X → Y"),
  Rate = c(24.4852, 11.2264),
  SE = c(10.1525, 4.1299)
)

knitr::kable(rate_tbl, digits = 3)


```

> Rate parameters summarize the average amount of change, that is, opportunities for tie updates, between consecutive waves. The estimated rate is higher for Wave V → Wave X (24.485) than for Wave X → Wave Y (11.226), suggesting that friendship nominations changed more intensively between the first two observed waves than between the later two waves..


> @tbl-siena-results shows the parameter estimates for the final model (Model 2).


```{r}
#| label: tbl-siena-results
#| tbl-cap: "RSiena parameter estimates for the final model."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
#| message: false
#| warning: false

library(dplyr)
library(tibble)
library(knitr)

build_est_df <- function(ans) {
  # Attempt 1: read directly from the fitted object (theta/se/tstat) + included effects
  effdf <- tryCatch(as.data.frame(ans$effects), error = function(e) NULL)

  if (!is.null(effdf) && "include" %in% names(effdf) && !is.null(ans$theta) && !is.null(ans$se)) {
    inc <- effdf$include

    # Prefer the set of requested effects if available (avoids extras)
    if ("requestedEffect" %in% names(effdf)) {
      inc <- inc & effdf$requestedEffect
    }

    lab <- NULL
    for (nm in c("effectName", "shortName", "functionName")) {
      if (nm %in% names(effdf)) { lab <- as.character(effdf[[nm]]); break }
    }
    if (is.null(lab)) lab <- rownames(effdf)

    lab <- lab[inc]

    th <- as.numeric(ans$theta)
    se <- as.numeric(ans$se)
    t <- th / se

    # If lengths match, we can safely bind
    if (length(lab) == length(th)) {
      return(tibble(Effect = lab, Estimate = th, SE = se, `t-ratio` = t))
    }
  }

  # Attempt 2 (fallback): parse the printed summary table
  txt <- capture.output(print(summary(ans)))

  # IMPORTANT: use double backslashes in R strings (\\s, \\d, etc.)
  rgx <- "^\\s*(.*?)\\s+(-?\\d*\\.?\\d+(?:[eE]-?\\d+)?|NA)\\s+(-?\\d*\\.?\\d+(?:[eE]-?\\d+)?|NA)\\s+(-?\\d*\\.?\\d+(?:[eE]-?\\d+)?|NA)\\s*$"

  out <- lapply(txt, function(line) {
    m <- regexec(rgx, line, perl = TRUE)
    r <- regmatches(line, m)[[1]]
    if (length(r) == 5) {
      tibble(
        Effect = trimws(r[2]),
        Estimate = as.numeric(r[3]),
        SE = as.numeric(r[4]),
        `t-ratio` = as.numeric(r[5])
      )
    } else {
      NULL
    }
  })

  out <- bind_rows(out)

  # Drop empty labels and obviously non-effect rows
  out %>%
    filter(Effect != "", !is.na(Estimate) | !is.na(SE) | !is.na(`t-ratio`))
}

effects_df <- build_est_df(res_final)

knitr::kable(effects_df, digits = 3)
```









## 9. Model Fit

>Goodness-of-fit (GoF) assesses whether the fitted SAOM can reproduce important structural features of the observed network. We simulate multiple networks from the fitted model and compare observed network statistics to the distribution of the same statistics in the simulated networks. If the observed statistics are not extreme relative to the simulated distribution (and the Monte Carlo p-values are not close to 0 or 1), the model is considered to fit those features reasonably well.
>
>Here, we evaluate GoF for the indegree andoutdegree distributions. We report GoF for the initial specification (Model 1) in @fig-gof-indeg-m1 and @fig-gof-outdeg-m1, and for the updated specification (Model 2, final) in @fig-gof-indeg-m2 and @fig-gof-outdeg-m2. @tbl-m1-m2-compare summarizes Monte Carlo p-values for Model 1 vs Model 2.



```{r}
#| label: gof-setup
#| include: false
#| message: false
#| warning: false
#| results: hide

library(RSiena)
library(tibble)
library(knitr)

dep_name <- "friendship_saom"

# Simulation settings for GoF:
gof_alg <- sienaAlgorithmCreate(n3 = 250, nsub = 0, seed = 12345)

# Simulate from Model 1
sims_m1 <- siena07(
  gof_alg,
  data = siena_dat,
  effects = eff_m1,
  prevAns = res_m1b,
  returnDeps = TRUE,
  batch = TRUE,
  silent = TRUE
)

max_deg <- min(15, nrow(netV_saom) - 1)
levs <- 0:max_deg

# GoF objects (Model 1)
gof_indeg_m1 <- sienaGOF(
  sims_m1, varName = dep_name,
  IndegreeDistribution, cumulative = FALSE, levls = levs
)

gof_outdeg_m1 <- sienaGOF(
  sims_m1, varName = dep_name,
  OutdegreeDistribution, cumulative = FALSE, levls = levs
)

# Robust p-value extraction
get_p <- function(gof_obj) {
  if (!is.null(gof_obj$p)) return(as.numeric(gof_obj$p))
  if (is.list(gof_obj) && length(gof_obj) > 0 && !is.null(gof_obj[[1]]$p)) return(as.numeric(gof_obj[[1]]$p))
  if (!is.null(attr(gof_obj, "p"))) return(as.numeric(attr(gof_obj, "p")))
  NA_real_
}

```





```{r}
#| label: fig-gof-indeg-m1
#| fig-cap: "Goodness-of-fit for the indegree distribution (Model 1)."
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 4.6
#| results: hide

invisible(capture.output(plot(gof_indeg_m1)))
```


```{r}
#| label: fig-gof-outdeg-m1
#| fig-cap: "Goodness-of-fit for the outdegree distribution (Model 1)."
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 4.6
#| results: hide

invisible(capture.output(plot(gof_outdeg_m1)))
```


```{r}
#| label: gof-model2
#| include: false


# Simulate for GoF under Model 2
sims_m2 <- siena07(
  gof_alg,
  data = siena_dat,
  effects = eff_m2,
  prevAns = res_m2,
  returnDeps = TRUE,
  batch = TRUE,
  silent = TRUE
)

gof_indeg_m2 <- sienaGOF(
  sims_m2, varName = dep_name,
  IndegreeDistribution, cumulative = FALSE, levls = levs
)

gof_outdeg_m2 <- sienaGOF(
  sims_m2, varName = dep_name,
  OutdegreeDistribution, cumulative = FALSE, levls = levs
)

```


```{r}
#| label: fig-gof-indeg-m2
#| fig-cap: "Goodness-of-fit for the indegree distribution (Model 2, final)."
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 4.6
#| results: hide

invisible(capture.output(plot(gof_indeg_m2)))
```

```{r}
#| label: fig-gof-outdeg-m2
#| fig-cap: "Goodness-of-fit for the outdegree distribution (Model 2, final)."
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 7
#| fig-height: 4.6
#| results: hide

invisible(capture.output(plot(gof_outdeg_m2)))
```




```{r}
#| label: tbl-m1-m2-compare
#| tbl-cap: "Key estimates comparison, Model 1 vs Model 2."
#| echo: false
#| results: asis
#| message: false
#| warning: false

est_m1 <- build_est_df(res_m1b)
est_m2 <- build_est_df(res_m2)

keep <- c("same", "transitive", "indegree - popularity", "outdegree - activity", "reciprocity")

m1_key <- est_m1 %>% filter(stringr::str_detect(tolower(Effect), paste(keep, collapse="|"))) %>%
  select(Effect, Estimate_M1 = Estimate, SE_M1 = SE, t_M1 = `t-ratio`)

m2_key <- est_m2 %>% filter(stringr::str_detect(tolower(Effect), paste(keep, collapse="|"))) %>%
  select(Effect, Estimate_M2 = Estimate, SE_M2 = SE, t_M2 = `t-ratio`)

cmp <- full_join(m1_key, m2_key, by = "Effect") %>%
  arrange(Effect)

knitr::kable(cmp, digits = 3)
```

## 10. Discussion and Limits




>We modeled best-friend nominations in classroom 09f across three waves (V, X, Y) using a stochastic actor-oriented model (SAOM) estimated on the constant node set (intersection sample; n = 25). We tested (H1) sex homophily (sameX), (H2) transitivity (transTrip), and (H3) a popularity-related mechanism (inPop). The final specification (Model 2) converged successfully, and the estimates can therefore be interpreted as evidence about tie formation and maintenance mechanisms conditional on the included controls.

>H1 (sex homophily). The estimate for sameX is positive but weak (Estimate = 0.246, t = 1.981). Because the t-ratio is close to conventional thresholds, we treat sex homophily as uncertain rather than as a robust finding in this classroom.

>H2 (transitivity). Transitive triplets are strongly positive (Estimate = 0.466, SE = 0.052, t = 8.988), indicating a strong tendency toward triadic closure in the objective function of the model. Conditional on reciprocity, degree-related effects, and baseline density, pupils are more likely to form ties to friends of friends, suggesting clustering in best-friend dynamics.

>H3 (popularity reinforcement). Contrary to the hypothesis, the indegree-related popularity effect is significantly negative (Estimate = −0.336, SE = 0.079, t = −4.274). Conditional on the other effects, pupils who already receive many nominations are less likely to receive additional nominations over time. This pattern is consistent with anti-preferential attachment under this operationalization. Given the nomination cap and classroom size, we avoid stronger behavioral claims beyond what the model directly supports.

>Other structural effects and controls. Reciprocity is large and positive (Estimate = 3.604, SE = 0.586, t = 6.150), indicating that nominations are much more likely to become mutual than under a baseline random process. The outdegree (density) parameter is negative (Estimate = −0.814, t = −1.895) and mainly captures overall sparsity rather than a substantive mechanism. Outdegree activity is positive (Estimate = 0.127, t = 2.929), suggesting heterogeneity in sending nominations. Reciprocity-related activity (Estimate = −0.460, t = −4.218) indicates that pupils with more reciprocated ties tend to reduce additional outgoing activity.

>Model fit and stability. Goodness-of-fit improved from Model 1 to Model 2, with Monte Carlo p-values increasing from 0.096 to 0.408 for the indegree distribution and from 0.000 to 0.172 for the outdegree distribution. These results indicate that the updated specification better reproduces observed degree distributions. Importantly, the key substantive effects remain stable across specifications, with inPop remaining negative (−0.376 to −0.336) and transTrip remaining strongly positive (0.350 to 0.466), suggesting that the main conclusions are not solely driven by the goodness-of-fit adjustment.

>Limitations. The analysis is restricted to 25 pupils observed in all three waves, which reduces statistical power and generalizability. Tie stability is moderate, indicating considerable turnover. Future work could explore additional covariates and alternative structural effects to further refine model fit.



